---
{state.status === "retired" ? <StateEvent view={state} /> : <BaseLayout title={`${event.title} | The Catch`} current="Events">
  <div slot="locator" class="locator"><a href="/events/">Series</a><a href="/events/miami-cargo-crash/">The Miami cargo crash</a><span>September 2026</span></div>
  <div class="content">
    <main class="story" id="story-root">
      <p class="story-kicker" data-layer="narrative">Miami International Airport &middot; September 6, 2026</p>
      <h1 data-layer="fact">{event.title}</h1>
      <p class="story-dek" data-layer="fact">{event.dek}</p>
      <ReadingModes />

      <nav class="story-toc" aria-label="In this story" data-layer="fact">
        <details class="toc-disclosure" open><summary><span>In this story</span><span class="toc-current" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
        <ol>
          <li><a href="#what-happened">What happened</a></li>
          <li><a href="#the-catch">The catch</a></li>
          <li><a href="#where-this-sits">Where this sits</a></li>
          <li><a href="#who-feels-it">Who feels it</a></li>
          <li><a href="#safety-history">The operator's safety history</a></li>
          <li><a href="#outlets">What the coverage got right, and what it got wrong</a></li>
          <li><a href="#claim-check">Claim checked against the record</a></li>
          <li><a href="#what-happened-next">What happened next</a></li>
          <li><a href="#unknowns">What we do not know yet</a></li>
          <li><a href="#records">The records</a></li>
        </ol>
      </details></nav>
      <div class="kpi-strip" data-layer="fact">
        {event.kpis.map((k) => (
          <div class="kpi">
            <span class="kpi-value">{k.value}<small>{k.unit ? ` ${k.unit}` : ""}</small></span>
            <span class="kpi-label">{k.label}</span>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>

      <aside class="three-things" data-layer="fact">
        <p class="tt-label">Three things to know</p>
        <ol>
          <li>The FAA said 21 Air Flight 7598, a Boeing 767-300 cargo jet from San Juan, Puerto Rico, overran the runway at Miami International Airport around 2 p.m. on Sunday, September 6.<Cite s="faa-statements-2026-09-06" passage="21 Air Flight 7598 overran the runway" /></li>
          <li>Five people died, all of them in a cleaning company's van on airport property. The Miami-Dade sheriff named them on Tuesday. The two pilots were treated and released. Three other people were still in hospital Tuesday, two of them in critical condition.<Cite s="local10-sheriff-2026-09-08" passage="Miami-Dade Sheriff Rosie Cordero-Stutz identified the five dead who were traveling in the Econoline van" /><Cite s="ap-victims-2026-09-08" passage="The pilot and co-pilot have been released from the hospital" /></li>
          <li>The FAA and 21 Air name 21 Air as the airline. Amazon's spokesperson called it an Amazon Air plane operated by 21 Air. The National Transportation Safety Board is investigating and has not given a cause.<Cite s="faa-statements-2026-09-06" /><Cite s="21air-statement-2026-09-06" /><Cite s="amazon-update-2026-09-06" passage="an Amazon Air plane operated by 21 Air" /><Cite s="ntsb-x-goteam-2026-09-06" /></li>
        </ol>
      </aside>
      <p class="railed rail-note" data-layer="fact">Story updated {event.updated}.</p>

      <div data-layer="proof" class="event-record-context">
        <p class="sources-line">This story rests on the FAA's written accident statement, official posts from the FAA, the NTSB, Miami International Airport, and Miami-Dade Fire Rescue, the operator's statement, Amazon's update page, a C-SPAN transcript of the Monday briefing, the NTSB's investigation page and docket, the FAA's airport data for Runway 30, the FAA's safety magazine, Flightradar24's tracking post, the airport's weather observation, the sheriff's Tuesday identifications as reported by Local 10 and the Associated Press, the FAA's own pages on arresting beds, a CBS News investigation of the operator's earlier safety complaints, and coverage checked against those records.</p>
      </div>

      <section id="what-happened">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">Sunday afternoon</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happened</h2>

        <SourcedBlock source="faa-statements-2026-09-06" kind="record">
          <p>The Federal Aviation Administration's written statement on aviation accidents, dated September 6, says 21 Air Flight 7598 overran the runway after landing at Miami International Airport around 2 p.m. local time. It identifies the airplane as a Boeing 767-300 cargo aircraft that had departed Luis Muñoz Marín International Airport in San Juan, Puerto Rico, and says the FAA will investigate.<Cite s="faa-statements-2026-09-06" passage="21 Air Flight 7598 overran the runway after landing at Miami International Airport around 2 p.m. local time on Sunday, Sept. 6." /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <p data-layer="narrative">The FAA's aviation-accidents page carries a September 6 entry that names the flight, the airplane, San Juan, and the 2 p.m. landing. The same words appear in the FAA's Sunday post on X.<Cite s="faa-statements-2026-09-06" passage="The FAA will investigate." /><Cite s="faa-x-7598-2026-09-06" passage="21 Air Flight 7598 overran the runway after landing at Miami International Airport around 2 p.m. local time on Sunday, Sept. 6." /></p>

        <SourcedBlock source="iflymia-x-groundstop-2026-09-06" kind="record">
          <p>Miami International Airport, posting as the Miami-Dade Aviation Department's airport account, said Prime Air Flight 7598 overran the airport's diagonal runway at about 2 p.m., leaving the aircraft disabled at the northwest end. All runways and taxiways were closed, and a full ground stop was in effect.<Cite s="iflymia-x-groundstop-2026-09-06" passage="All MIA runways and taxiways are currently closed, and a full ground stop is in effect." /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <p data-layer="narrative">The airport used the Prime Air flight number. The FAA used 21 Air. Those are two names for the same landing: a cargo flight flown for Amazon's air network by 21 Air, a cargo airline.<Cite s="iflymia-x-groundstop-2026-09-06" passage="Prime Air Flight 7598" /><Cite s="faa-statements-2026-09-06" passage="21 Air Flight 7598" /><Cite s="21air-statement-2026-09-06" passage="the accident involving one of our aircraft" /></p>

        <SourcedBlock source="mdfr-x-update1-2026-09-06" kind="record">
          <p>At 3:25 p.m., Miami-Dade Fire Rescue said more than 60 of its units were on scene of a plane that overran a runway and struck multiple vehicles. Crews found an airplane on fire, with heavy flames and smoke.<Cite s="mdfr-x-update1-2026-09-06" passage="Over 60 Miami-Dade Fire Rescue units are on scene" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="mdfr-x-update2-2026-09-06" kind="record">
          <p>By 6:25 p.m. the department said it had received the call at 1:55 p.m. More than 60 units and about 200 personnel responded. The plane had stopped beyond the airport operational boundary. Firefighters used airport foam on a complex engine-compartment fire, shut down the aircraft's engine, and worked a fuel leak. One person trapped under a vehicle was extricated. Five patients were taken to local hospitals.<Cite s="mdfr-x-update2-2026-09-06" passage="MDFR transported five patients to local area hospitals." /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="abc-miami-cargo-crash" kind="record">
          <p>Miami-Dade County Mayor Daniella Levine Cava said at a news conference Sunday evening that five people were dead and five were injured. ABC News recorded that statement. The fire department's own Sunday posts do not give a death count.<Cite s="abc-miami-cargo-crash" passage="Five people are dead and five people are injured" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="mdc-mayor-2026-09-07" kind="record">
          <p>On Monday the mayor's office issued its own release. Levine Cava wrote that she had spoken directly with NTSB Chairwoman Jennifer Homendy ahead of Homendy's briefing at the airport, which the mayor attended. She thanked Miami-Dade Fire Rescue teams who, she wrote, responded in less than 30 seconds to extinguish the fire and care for the injured, and she noted assistance to stranded passengers.<Cite s="mdc-mayor-2026-09-07" passage="our Miami-Dade Fire Rescue teams, who responded to the scene in less than 30 seconds to extinguish the fire and care for the injured" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="ntsb-x-goteam-2026-09-06" kind="record">
          <p>The National Transportation Safety Board said it had launched a go team for a runway excursion involving a Boeing 767-300 cargo airplane operated as 21 Air Flight 7598 from San Juan to Miami. Chairwoman Jennifer Homendy would be the board member on scene. A media briefing was set for Monday.<Cite s="ntsb-x-goteam-2026-09-06" passage="NTSB has launched a go team to investigate Sunday's runway excursion accident" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="ntsb-dca26ma352-page" kind="record">
          <p>The NTSB's investigation page, saved Tuesday, gives the case the number DCA26MA352 and lists its status as ongoing. It says 21 Air LLC Flight 7598, a Boeing 767-33A, overran the end of Runway 30 during landing, and that the flight was a cargo flight from San Juan flown under Part 121, the federal rules that govern scheduled airlines. The public docket, where the NTSB posts interview summaries and recorder reports, has not been released.<Cite s="ntsb-dca26ma352-page" passage="Flight was a 14 Code of Federal Regulations Part 121 cargo flight from San Juan, Puerto Rico (SJU) to MIA." /><Cite s="ntsb-dca26ma352-page" passage="Status: Ongoing" /><Cite s="ntsb-dca26ma352-docket" passage="The docket for this investigation has not been released." /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="21air-statement-2026-09-06" kind="record">
          <p>21 Air, on its website, said it was devastated by the accident involving one of its aircraft in Miami on September 6. Chief executive Keith Winters wrote that the company was cooperating with the NTSB, the FAA, and local authorities, and that the circumstances were under investigation. The statement acknowledges people who lost their lives. It does not give a number.<Cite s="21air-statement-2026-09-06" passage="We are devastated by the accident involving one of our aircraft in Miami on 09/06." /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="amazon-update-2026-09-06" kind="record">
          <p>Amazon's company-news page holds two statements from spokesperson Kelly Nantel. At 1:58 p.m. Pacific time she confirmed "an Amazon Air plane operated by 21 Air" had an incident while attempting to land. At 3:05 p.m. Pacific time she wrote that five people had lost their lives, and that the airplane, operated by 21 Air, was attempting to land when the incident occurred.<Cite s="amazon-update-2026-09-06" passage="The airplane, which was operated by 21 Air, was attempting to land when this incident occurred." /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="amazon-x-7598-2026-09-06" kind="record">
          <p>Amazon News posted the earlier 1:58 p.m. Pacific statement on X the same afternoon.<Cite s="amazon-x-7598-2026-09-06" passage="We can confirm that an Amazon Air plane operated by 21 Air experienced an incident while attempting to land at Miami International Airport today." /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="duffy-x-7598-2026-09-06" kind="record">
          <p>Transportation Secretary Sean Duffy wrote that a full ground stop was in effect at Miami International while first responders assessed the scene, and that people traveling through the area should expect delays and possible cancellations. He said FAA inspectors would investigate.<Cite s="duffy-x-7598-2026-09-06" passage="We’ve issued a full ground stop at Miami International while first responders assess the scene after an Amazon cargo plane overran the runway." /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Flightradar24's incident post names the airplane as registration N1997A. The FAA aircraft registry lists N1997A as a Boeing 767-33A, serial 27310, registered to Andromeda Leasing II LLC of Purchase, New York.<Cite s="fr24-21air-7598-miami" passage="registration N1997A" /><Cite s="faa-registry-n1997a" passage="ANDROMEDA LEASING II LLC" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Where 21 Air sits depends on who is describing it. Homendy called it 21 Air LLC based in Miami, and the Reuters dispatch on her briefing called it Miami-based. CBS, in its Tuesday story on the company, called it a North Carolina company. We hold no corporate filing that settles the question, and a search of the CBS story we saved turns up no city in North Carolina. What is not in dispute is that the Transportation Department certified 21 Air in 2016 for foreign charter cargo.<Cite s="cspan-ntsb-briefing-transcript-2026-09-07" passage="operated by 21 Air LLC based in Miami" /><Cite s="spokesman-reuters-blackboxes-2026-09-07" passage="operated by Miami-based 21 Air LLC" /><Cite s="cbs-21air-safety-record-2026-09-08" passage="was operated by a North Carolina company" /><Cite s="dot-order-2016-9-22" passage="Served: September 30, 2016" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">The airplane was {aircraft.ageYears} years old. Flightradar24 says it flew for various airlines as a passenger carrier from {aircraft.mfrYear} to {aircraft.convertedYear}, when it was converted to cargo configuration, and the FAA registry gives the same manufacture year. Asked at the briefing about the airplane's age, Homendy said she believed it was manufactured in 1994 and that age and ownership history are always looked at. Reuters described it as a 32-year-old Boeing 767-33A. Age by itself is not evidence of an unsafe aircraft: airliners are maintained on a schedule rather than retired on a birthday, and nothing has been published about this airplane's condition. What the age and the conversion do is name records investigators will read, which is the maintenance history, the conversion paperwork, and the write-ups from the earlier flights that day.<Cite s="fr24-21air-7598-miami" passage="The 32 year old aircraft flew with various airlines as a passenger carrier from 1994 to 2015, when it was converted to cargo configuration." /><Cite s="faa-registry-n1997a" passage="MFR Year 1994" /><Cite s="cspan-ntsb-briefing-transcript-2026-09-07" passage="I believe it was manufactured in 1994." /><Cite s="spokesman-reuters-blackboxes-2026-09-07" passage="The 32-year-old Boeing 767-33A, operated by Miami-based 21 Air LLC" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="the-catch">
        <aside class="the-catch" data-layer="fact">
          <p class="tt-label">The catch</p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <span class="catch-tag">Told versus record</span>
              <h3>Flown by 21 Air, not by Amazon.</h3>
              Three names belong to this airplane. The FAA registry lists the owner as Andromeda Leasing II. The NTSB names 21 Air LLC as the operator, the airline whose pilots flew it. Amazon calls it an Amazon Air plane operated by 21 Air: Amazon was the customer. "Amazon cargo plane" is fair shorthand for the customer. "Operated by Amazon," as NPR quoted the sheriff, is wrong.<Cite s="faa-registry-n1997a" passage="ANDROMEDA LEASING II LLC" /><Cite s="ntsb-dca26ma352-page" passage="21 Air LLC Flight 7598, a Boeing 767-33A, overran the end of Runway 30" /><Cite s="amazon-update-2026-09-06" passage="an Amazon Air plane operated by 21 Air" /><Cite s="npr-miami-cargo-crash" passage="operated by Amazon" />
              <a class="catch-jump" href="#claim-check">Claim check</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="catch-tag">Told versus record</span>
              <h3>Nobody on the plane died.</h3>
              Homendy said everyone killed was in two vehicles on the ground: a cleaning company's van on airport property and a car on Northwest 67th Avenue. The two crew survived.<Cite s="cbs-miami-cargo-ntsb" passage="Homendy said all victims were in those two vehicles." /><Cite s="reuters-miami-cargo-ntsb" passage="The two people aboard the plane, the pilot and co-pilot, survived" /><Cite s="cspan-ntsb-briefing-transcript-2026-09-07" passage="2012 white Ford Econoline van" />
              <a class="catch-jump" href="#who-feels-it">Who feels it</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="catch-tag">Left out</span>
              <h3>122 beds at 70 airports, not 120 airports.</h3>
              Homendy said Runway 30 has no engineered materials arresting system, a bed of crushable material meant to stop a plane that leaves the pavement. The FAA's own magazine counts {emasCount.installations} such beds at {emasCount.airports} U.S. airports, and the FAA's blog counts the same {emasCount.installations} by runway end at the same {emasCount.airports} airports; Homendy said about 72. The Associated Press, and CNBC carrying the same sentence, wrote "more than 120 airports," which swaps beds for airports.<Cite s="cspan-ntsb-briefing-transcript-2026-09-07" passage="the lack thereof of an engineered materials arresting system or EMASS" /><Cite s="faa-safety-briefing-2026-03" passage="EMAS installations at 70 U.S. airports." /><Cite s="faa-blog-runway-safety" passage="EMAS is now at 122 runway ends at 70 airports in more than two dozen U.S. states." /><Cite s="cspan-ntsb-briefing-transcript-2026-09-07" passage="about 72 airports across the US" /><Cite s="ap-miami-cargo-crash" passage="installed at more than 120 airports" />
              <a class="catch-jump" href="#what-happened-next">What happened next</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="catch-tag">Left out</span>
              <h3>What the video shows.</h3>
              Outside analysts watching public video described a long float with a tailwind. Flightradar24's tracking data put the airplane at {adsb.exitKnots} knots leaving the usable runway and {adsb.lastKnots} knots at the last point received. The NTSB has confirmed none of this and is holding its own speed data until it matches the flight data recorder.<Cite s="pbs-miami-cargo-what-went-wrong" passage="They landed at about 185 miles an hour" /><Cite s="fr24-21air-7598-miami" passage="speed at 112 knots as it exited the usable area of the runway. The last received speed was 69 knots" /><Cite s="cspan-ntsb-briefing-transcript-2026-09-07" passage="we want to confirm that data with the flight data recorder" />
              <a class="catch-jump" href="#where-this-sits">Where this sits</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="catch-tag">Who pays</span>
              <h3>More than 225 flights cancelled over two days.</h3>
              Officials counted more than 225 cancellations Sunday and Monday, with hundreds more delayed, the Associated Press reported Tuesday. Earlier snapshots were smaller because they were earlier: more than 160 cancelled by late Sunday, at least 55 more by Monday morning. Two of four runways were still closed Tuesday.<Cite s="ap-victims-2026-09-08" passage="More than 225 flights were canceled Sunday and Monday" /><Cite s="ap-miami-cargo-crash" passage="More than 160 flights were canceled and nearly 325 delayed by late Sunday, according to FlightAware" /><Cite s="usatoday-miami-cargo-day2" passage="At least 55 flights have been cancelled, and more than 100 others are delayed" /><Cite s="local10-sheriff-2026-09-08" passage="Two of Miami International Airport’s four runways remained closed on Monday and Tuesday" />
              <a class="catch-jump" href="#who-feels-it">Who feels it</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="catch-tag">Told versus record</span>
              <h3>Van year: 2012.</h3>
              CNBC wrote 2021. Homendy, on camera, said 2012.<Cite s="cspan-ntsb-briefing-transcript-2026-09-07" passage="2012 white Ford Econoline van" /><Cite s="cnbc-miami-cargo-ntsb" passage="2021 white Ford Econoline van" />
              <a class="catch-jump" href="#claim-check">Claim check</a>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </aside>
      </section>

      <section id="where-this-sits">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">The afternoon, in order</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Where this sits</h2>

        <SourcedBlock source="iflymia-x-groundstop-2026-09-06" kind="record">
          <p>The airport's first public post closed every runway and taxiway. Later Sunday it said one runway was open, then two of four, with delays still in effect. Passengers were told to check with their airline before coming to the airport.<Cite s="iflymia-x-groundstop-2026-09-06" /><Cite s="iflymia-x-one-runway-2026-09-06" passage="One runway is currently open" /><Cite s="iflymia-x-two-runways-2026-09-06" passage="Two of our four runways are currently open" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <p data-layer="narrative">That sequence is the operational story of the day: a full stop after the overrun, then a partial reopening while wreckage, fuel, and the investigation still occupied the northwest end of the field.<Cite s="iflymia-x-two-runways-2026-09-06" passage="we are still experiencing impacts from this incident" /><Cite s="mdfr-x-update2-2026-09-06" passage="ongoing fuel leak" /></p>

        <SourcedBlock source="ntsb-x-wreckage-2026-09-07" kind="record">
          <p>On Monday the NTSB identified the runway as Runway 30 and the operator as 21 Air LLC Flight 7598.<Cite s="ntsb-x-wreckage-2026-09-07" passage="overran the end of Runway 30 while landing Sunday at Miami International Airport" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="cspan-ntsb-briefing-transcript-2026-09-07" kind="record">
          <p>Homendy said the airplane traveled about 1,300 feet past the paved surface. CBS, Reuters, and CNBC all carried the figure from that one briefing. That is 396 meters, converting 1,300 feet at 0.3048 meters per foot and rounding to the nearest meter.<Cite s="cspan-ntsb-briefing-transcript-2026-09-07" passage="In total it was about 1300 ft. past the paved surface." /><Cite s="cbs-miami-cargo-ntsb" passage="In total, the plane traveled about 1,300 feet past the paved surface" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <details class="receipt" data-layer="proof"><summary>how 396 meters was computed</summary>
          <p>1,300 feet times 0.3048 meters per foot equals 396.24 meters, rounded to {overrunDistance.meters}. The 1,300-foot figure is Homendy's, in the C-SPAN transcript of the Monday briefing; the three outlets that carried it are one lineage from that briefing, not three confirmations. The NTSB's own saved posts do not include the distance.</p>
        </details>

        <SourcedBlock source="noaa-metar-kmia-2026-09-06" kind="record">
          <p>At 1:53 p.m. Eastern, the time Homendy gave for the accident, the airport's own weather observation reported a thunderstorm, wind from the south at 17 knots gusting to 26, a peak gust of 26 knots one minute earlier, and a wind shift at 1:37 p.m. The NTSB has not said weather played a part.<Cite s="noaa-metar-kmia-2026-09-06" passage="METAR KMIA 061753Z 19017G26KT 10SM TS SCT020CB BKN048 BKN150 BKN250 30/22 A2994 RMK AO2 PK WND 18026/1752 WSHFT 1737" /><Cite s="cspan-ntsb-briefing-transcript-2026-09-07" passage="an accident that occurred yesterday at 1:53 p.m. Eastern Daylight Time" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <details class="receipt" data-layer="proof"><summary>how that observation was read</summary>
          <p>Saved NOAA aviation-weather METAR for KMIA, line beginning METAR KMIA 061753Z. 1753Z is 1:53 p.m. Eastern Daylight Time on September 6. 19017G26KT is wind from 190 degrees at 17 knots, gusting 26. TS is thunderstorm. PK WND 18026/1752 is a peak wind of 26 knots from 180 degrees at 1752Z. WSHFT 1737 is a wind shift at 1737Z, 1:37 p.m. Eastern. The line was saved on September 8 from a 72-hour request that still held the September 6 observation.</p>
        </details>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Miles O'Brien, PBS NewsHour's aviation correspondent, described the public video as a landing with a tailwind that also had a crosswind component, at about 185 miles an hour, about halfway down a 9,000-foot runway, and said a go-around would have been the move once the airplane was floating halfway down. Mary Schiavo, a pilot and former Transportation Department inspector general, told CNBC the video shows the plane floating above the runway without flaring; she said it was not raining, with dark storm clouds and strong winds reported. CBS, covering Homendy, said the NTSB team would include groups for meteorology and aircraft performance. The NTSB has not said any of this. O'Brien and Schiavo are two separate readings of the same public video; Schiavo's words reached CNBC through an Associated Press dispatch, so AP and CNBC are one source for her, not two.<Cite s="pbs-miami-cargo-what-went-wrong" passage="They landed at about 185 miles an hour" /><Cite s="cnbc-miami-cargo-crash-probe" passage="floating above the runway for an extended period without flaring" /><Cite s="ap-miami-cargo-crash" passage="floating above the runway for an extended period without flaring" /><Cite s="cbs-miami-cargo-ntsb" passage="aircraft performance, structures, meteorology and airport survival factors" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Halfway down a 9,000-foot runway, in O'Brien's phrase, needs a denominator. The FAA's airport data lists Runway 30 at {runway30.lengthFeet.toLocaleString("en-US")} feet of pavement, but only {runway30.landingDistanceAvailableFeet.toLocaleString("en-US")} feet of that is declared available for landing. Halfway down the pavement and halfway down the landing distance are about {halfwayGapFeet} feet apart.<Cite s="pbs-miami-cargo-what-went-wrong" passage="They landed at about 185 miles an hour" /><Cite s="faa-aip-florida" passage="True Dimensions: 9360 ft x 150 ft" /><Cite s="faa-aip-florida" passage="Landing Distance Available: 7913" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">One speed measurement is already public. Flightradar24, reading the airplane's own broadcast position data, put it at {adsb.exitKnots} knots as it left the usable runway and {adsb.lastKnots} knots at the last point received. Those numbers come from the airplane's transponder, not from the flight data recorder, and the NTSB has not confirmed them: Homendy said Monday the board had speed data from various sources and would hold it until it was checked against the recorder. Flightradar24 calls the figure the aircraft's speed from the tracking data it received. Its post does not say whether that is speed over the ground or speed through the air, and the difference matters on a day with a 17-knot wind, so this page does not label it either way. They do not contradict O'Brien's 185 miles an hour, which describes touchdown, a different point on the runway.<Cite s="fr24-21air-7598-miami" passage="speed at 112 knots as it exited the usable area of the runway. The last received speed was 69 knots" /><Cite s="cspan-ntsb-briefing-transcript-2026-09-07" passage="We do have data, but it's from various sources, and we want to confirm that data with the flight data recorder" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">The precedent that shares the customer is not the precedent that shares the mechanism. On February 23, 2019, Atlas Air Flight 3591, a Boeing 767 flying cargo for Amazon, crashed near Houston after leaving Miami: a different operator, a different phase of flight, and a different cause. The NTSB's closer file is its May 2026 report on runway overruns, drawn from 11 overruns between 2008 and 2022 in which airplanes landed on wet runways and stopped later than their braking assumptions predicted. Whether Runway 30 was wet at 1:53 p.m. is not established: the airport observation reported a thunderstorm, and Schiavo, watching the video, said it was not raining.<Cite s="ntsb-atlas-dca19ma086" passage="Atlas operated the airplane as a Title 14 Code of Federal Regulations Part 121 domestic cargo flight for Amazon.com Services LLC" /><Cite s="ntsb-dca25sr002-page" passage="11 runway overrun accidents and incidents from a 15-year period, 2008–2022, that occurred after the airplanes involved landed on wet runways" /><Cite s="noaa-metar-kmia-2026-09-06" passage="METAR KMIA 061753Z 19017G26KT 10SM TS" /><Cite s="cnbc-miami-cargo-crash-probe" passage="floating above the runway for an extended period without flaring" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="who-feels-it">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">People on the ground, and people in the air</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Who feels it</h2>

        <SourcedBlock source="cspan-ntsb-briefing-transcript-2026-09-07" kind="record">
          <p>Homendy said Monday, in the C-SPAN transcript of the briefing, that the airplane overran Runway 30, struck navigational aids, then hit a 2012 white Ford Econoline van owned by Professional Ocean Service Corporation, a contract cleaning company, with seven people inside, on airport property. The airplane then went through a perimeter fence and hit a Toyota Corolla Cross LE on Northwest 67th Avenue. CBS, quoting Homendy, said everyone killed was in those two vehicles. Reuters reported that she said the two people on the airplane survived and had not yet been interviewed.<Cite s="cspan-ntsb-briefing-transcript-2026-09-07" passage="Then impacted a 2012 white Ford Econoline van." /><Cite s="cbs-miami-cargo-ntsb" passage="Homendy said all victims were in those two vehicles." /><Cite s="reuters-miami-cargo-ntsb" passage="The two people aboard the plane, the pilot and co-pilot, survived" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Sunday night that was not yet known. Miami officials told reporters it was too soon to say whether the dead were on the airplane or in the vehicles. The fire chief said the pilot and copilot had been trapped in the cockpit. By Monday Homendy's account put the deaths in the two vehicles and the two crew on the airplane among the living.<Cite s="pbs-miami-cargo-crash" passage="too soon to determine whether the people who died were on board the plane or in the vehicles" /><Cite s="nbc-miami-cargo-crash" passage="retrieve the pilot and co-pilot, trapped in the plane" /><Cite s="reuters-miami-cargo-ntsb" passage="The two people aboard the plane, the pilot and co-pilot, survived" /></p>

        <SourcedBlock source="local10-sheriff-2026-09-08" kind="record">
          <p>On Tuesday morning Sheriff Rosie Cordero-Stutz named the dead. All five were in the van: Rolando Aleman Leon, 55; Yoel Rodriguez Naranjo, 53; Julio C. Pineda, 75; Carlos Acosta Fajardo, 53; and Javierkys Reyes Quevedo, 47. The sheriff's office named the three people still in hospital as Eugenio Corredor, in critical condition; Ridoel Averhoff Diaz, 32, in critical condition; and Roosevelt Sebastian Perdomo Torres, 36, in stable condition. The two pilots, Jaime Felipe Silva Molina, 37, and Joseph Carol, 55, had minor injuries and were released.<Cite s="local10-sheriff-2026-09-08" passage="Miami-Dade Sheriff Rosie Cordero-Stutz identified the five dead who were traveling in the Econoline van as Rolando Aleman Leon, 55; Yoel Rodriguez Naranjo, 53; Julio C. Pineda, 75; Carlos Acosta Fajardo, 53; and Javierkys Reyes Quevedo, 47." /><Cite s="local10-sheriff-2026-09-08" passage="MDSO identified the pilots as Jaime Felipe Silva Molina, 37, and Joseph Carol, 55, who both suffered minor injuries." /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="ap-victims-2026-09-08" kind="record">
          <p>The Associated Press, reporting the same briefing Tuesday, said the five were all in a van owned by Professional Ocean Service Corporation, the cleaning company Homendy had named, and that the company would not comment out of respect for the families. AP's story carries a correction: an earlier version misspelled Rodriguez Naranjo's last name because the sheriff's news release had it wrong. The sheriff's count of seven people in the van, the three in hospital, and the driver of the Toyota on Northwest 67th Avenue do not yet add up to a public list of who was in which vehicle.<Cite s="ap-victims-2026-09-08" passage="The five people killed were all in a van owned by Professional Ocean Service Corp., a company that cleans planes." /><Cite s="ap-victims-2026-09-08" passage="an earlier version of this story misspelled the last name of victim Yoel Rodriguez Naranjo" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="mdfr-x-update2-2026-09-06" kind="record">
          <p>Fire rescue took five patients to hospitals Sunday. CBS, quoting the Miami-Dade Sheriff's Office, said two of the injured were taken to Jackson Memorial Hospital and the other three to Jackson's Ryder Trauma Center in critical condition. NBC, quoting Fire Chief Raied Jadallah, had said Sunday that three were in critical condition at a trauma center and two went to a local hospital. By Tuesday one of the three had been listed as stable and the two pilots had gone home. The five injured are not five more people beyond the five dead, and they are not all people on the ground: Reuters reported five injured, three of them critically, and by Tuesday that count resolves into the {injured.pilots} pilots plus the {injured.ground} people still in hospital.<Cite s="mdfr-x-update2-2026-09-06" passage="MDFR transported five patients to local area hospitals." /><Cite s="spokesman-reuters-blackboxes-2026-09-07" passage="Five people were injured, three of them critically" /><Cite s="cbs-miami-cargo-ntsb" passage="Two of the injured victims were transported to Jackson Memorial Hospital" /><Cite s="nbc-miami-cargo-crash" passage="Three people are in critical condition" /><Cite s="local10-sheriff-2026-09-08" passage="The two pilots were treated and have since been released" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="iflymia-x-two-runways-2026-09-06" kind="record">
          <p>Travelers through Miami spent Sunday under a ground stop, then a partial reopening with two of four runways open. The Associated Press, citing FlightAware, wrote that more than 160 flights were canceled and nearly 325 delayed by late Sunday. USA Today, writing Monday during the Labor Day travel rush and also citing FlightAware, wrote that at least 55 flights had been cancelled and more than 100 others delayed.<Cite s="iflymia-x-two-runways-2026-09-06" passage="Passengers with flights scheduled today should check directly with their airline" /><Cite s="ap-miami-cargo-crash" passage="More than 160 flights were canceled and nearly 325 delayed by late Sunday, according to FlightAware" /><Cite s="usatoday-miami-cargo-day2" passage="during the Labor Day travel rush" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="mia-home-2026-09-08" kind="record">
          <p>The airport's homepage, saved September 8, still said two of four runways remained closed, that cancellations continued because aircraft and crews had not arrived, and that passengers with flights that day should check with their airline before heading to the airport.<Cite s="mia-home-2026-09-08" passage="Cancellations continue due to aircraft and flight crews not having arrived." /></p></SourcedBlock>
      </section>

      <section id="safety-history">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">Before this flight</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">The operator's safety history</h2>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Homendy said Monday that the investigation would look at 21 Air's own policies and procedures, the contract with Amazon, and the FAA's oversight of both. On Tuesday CBS News published an account of the operator's record before this flight, drawn from legal documents and interviews. None of it is a finding about Flight 7598, and this page keeps it in four parts, in order: what stands on the record of a legal proceeding, what former employees allege, what management and the federal safety agency said back, and what nobody has established.<Cite s="cspan-ntsb-briefing-transcript-2026-09-07" passage="21 Air LLC policies and procedures, their relationship, the contractual relationship with Amazon. We'll also look at FAA oversight of all involved operators" /><Cite s="cbs-21air-safety-record-2026-09-08" passage="CBS News has learned from records and interviews" /></p>

        <SourcedBlock source="cbs-21air-safety-record-2026-09-08" kind="record">
          <p><strong>On the record in a legal proceeding.</strong> Karl Seuring, a 21 Air pilot, filed a complaint saying he was fired for raising safety concerns. His appeal led to a 2023 Department of Labor tribunal, a hearing before a federal judge, and CBS reports the case is still pending. In that proceeding a former 21 Air chief pilot, Bruce Joseph, testified in March 2024 that he had "personal knowledge of people being discouraged" from logging safety concerns in the company system. He testified that the then-director of safety told him "don't put anything" in the safety software before speaking to the chief executive, and that asked whether reports eventually got filed he answered: "Most of it did not." Seuring's attorney told the judge that another pilot "was being pressured to fly in periods of time that were too short to comply with the proper amounts of rest, and that implicated safety."<Cite s="cbs-21air-safety-record-2026-09-08" passage="His appeal led to a 2023 Department of Labor tribunal" /><Cite s="cbs-21air-safety-record-2026-09-08" passage="where the case is still pending" /><Cite s="cbs-21air-safety-record-2026-09-08" passage="personal knowledge of people being discouraged" /><Cite s="cbs-21air-safety-record-2026-09-08" passage="don't put anything" /><Cite s="cbs-21air-safety-record-2026-09-08" passage="Most of it did not." /><Cite s="cbs-21air-safety-record-2026-09-08" passage="was being pressured to fly in periods of time that were too short to comply with the proper amounts of rest, and that implicated safety" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="cbs-21air-safety-record-2026-09-08" kind="record">
          <p><strong>What former employees allege.</strong> These are allegations by named people, not findings by anyone. CBS reports that "Seuring and other former 21 Air pilots claimed managers suppressed safety reports, pushed crew to fly without proper rest, staffed pilots who could not communicate safely in English, and put faulty planes in the sky, documents show." Seuring told CBS: "We've had fuel leak issues. We've had radar problems and circuit breakers, smoke in the cockpit." He added that "Pilots were coached on how the write-ups should be done." A retired 21 Air pilot, Tony Bless, who testified in Seuring's case, "claimed the company would routinely defer maintenance, and that things would often go wrong on its aging converted passenger planes." Don Helmig, a former fighter pilot who had been reviewing safety policy for the company, resigned in June 2021 in an email to the owner saying that in his opinion what the company wished him to do was to "say nothing, but that is not how Safety is supposed to operate, by paying 'lip service to SMS' [Safety Management Systems] then doing exactly nothing."<Cite s="cbs-21air-safety-record-2026-09-08" passage="Seuring and other former 21 Air pilots claimed managers suppressed safety reports, pushed crew to fly without proper rest, staffed pilots who could not communicate safely in English, and put faulty planes in the sky, documents show." /><Cite s="cbs-21air-safety-record-2026-09-08" passage="We've had fuel leak issues. We've had radar problems and circuit breakers, smoke in the cockpit" /><Cite s="cbs-21air-safety-record-2026-09-08" passage="Pilots were coached on how the write-ups should be done." /><Cite s="cbs-21air-safety-record-2026-09-08" passage="claimed the company would routinely defer maintenance, and that things would often go wrong on its aging converted passenger planes" /><Cite s="cbs-21air-safety-record-2026-09-08" passage="say nothing, but that is not how Safety is supposed to operate, by paying 'lip service to SMS' [Safety Management Systems] then doing exactly nothing." /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="cbs-21air-safety-record-2026-09-08" kind="record">
          <p><strong>The denials, and what OSHA did.</strong> The Occupational Safety and Health Administration rejected Seuring's complaint over the firing he called retaliatory; the tribunal exists because he appealed that rejection. Mike Mendez, 21 Air's chief executive at the time, testified that Joseph "was fired from 21 Air and left under very bad circumstances" and that he believed the former chief pilot was inventing damaging claims. Mendez denied retaliation and testified: "My responsibility is to make sure we run a safe airline, not to hurt people and not to hide anything." A former fleet manager Seuring named, Amir Anoshiravani, denied the incident described and told CBS he had "never come in contact" with Seuring. 21 Air did not answer CBS's requests for comment; its own website carries the statement that it is devastated by the accident and cooperating with the investigation.<Cite s="cbs-21air-safety-record-2026-09-08" passage="The Occupational Safety and Health Administration (OSHA) rejected Seuring's complaint" /><Cite s="cbs-21air-safety-record-2026-09-08" passage="was fired from 21 Air and left under very bad circumstances" /><Cite s="cbs-21air-safety-record-2026-09-08" passage="My responsibility is to make sure we run a safe airline, not to hurt people and not to hide anything" /><Cite s="cbs-21air-safety-record-2026-09-08" passage="never come in contact" /><Cite s="cbs-21air-safety-record-2026-09-08" passage="21 Air did not respond to CBS News' requests for comment." /><Cite s="21air-statement-2026-09-06" passage="We are devastated by the accident involving one of our aircraft in Miami on 09/06." /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <p data-layer="narrative">What nobody has established is a link between any of that and this landing. CBS says so in its own story: "It is not known whether past safety issues had any connection to the Sunday incident in Miami." The NTSB has published no probable cause, has released nothing from the recorders, and said Monday it would not determine cause while still on scene. What the history does establish is that the policies Homendy said the board will examine have been contested before, by named people, in a proceeding that has not finished.<Cite s="cbs-21air-safety-record-2026-09-08" passage="It is not known whether past safety issues had any connection to the Sunday incident in Miami." /><Cite s="cspan-ntsb-briefing-transcript-2026-09-07" passage="We will not be determining probable cause while we are on scene." /></p>

        <SourcedBlock source="cbs-21air-safety-record-2026-09-08" kind="record">
          <p><strong>Who controls 21 Air, and what Amazon says.</strong> CBS reports that "The cargo airline hired by Amazon, 21 Air, is controlled by Houston Astros owner Jim Crane through an LLC," and that the 2021 resignation email went to Crane as majority owner. Asked about the allegations, an Amazon spokesperson told CBS: "We'll defer to the NTSB regarding the circumstances of their investigation and to 21 Air regarding these allegations that pre-date our relationship with the company."<Cite s="cbs-21air-safety-record-2026-09-08" passage="The cargo airline hired by Amazon, 21 Air, is controlled by Houston Astros owner Jim Crane through an LLC." /><Cite s="cbs-21air-safety-record-2026-09-08" passage="Former fighter pilot Don Helmig wrote a resignation email to 21 Air majority owner Jim Crane in June 2021" /><Cite s="cbs-21air-safety-record-2026-09-08" passage="We'll defer to the NTSB regarding the circumstances of their investigation and to 21 Air regarding these allegations that pre-date our relationship with the company." /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <p data-layer="narrative">That answer leaves the oversight questions open, and they are open, not implied. When Amazon checked the company before or during the relationship, and whether that check reached the Labor Department case, is not public. Which side of the contract controlled maintenance and which controlled crew scheduling is not public; Homendy said the board wants to see who does what and what safety provisions the contract carries. Whether delivery requirements shaped how the flying was done is not public. What audit rights or termination provisions the contract carries is not public. No document answering any of those is in the records this page holds.<Cite s="cbs-21air-safety-record-2026-09-08" passage="these allegations that pre-date our relationship with the company" /><Cite s="cspan-ntsb-briefing-transcript-2026-09-07" passage="we're going to want to see what that relationship is, who does what" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="outlets">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">The coverage</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What the coverage got right, and what it got wrong</h2>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Sunday's coverage was checked against the FAA, airport, fire, and NTSB posts. Monday's briefing details were checked first against the C-SPAN transcript of what Homendy said, then against CBS, Reuters, and CNBC. Tuesday's identifications were checked against Local 10's report of the sheriff's briefing and the Associated Press. Two things to know about the coverage list: PBS's Sunday story is Associated Press copy under AP bylines, and CNBC's Monday story repeats AP's Mary Schiavo quotation and AP's arresting-bed sentence word for word. Where this page says AP and another outlet agree, check whether they are the same dispatch.<Cite s="faa-statements-2026-09-06" /><Cite s="cspan-ntsb-briefing-transcript-2026-09-07" passage="Amazon Air Flight 7598, a Boeing 767-33 Alpha" /><Cite s="pbs-miami-cargo-crash" passage="Anthony Izaguirre, Associated Press" /><Cite s="cnbc-miami-cargo-crash-probe" passage="installed at more than 120 airports" /></p>

        <SourcedBlock source="abc-miami-cargo-crash" kind="checked">
          <p><strong>ABC</strong> quoted the FAA statement on flight 7598, the Boeing 767-300, San Juan, and the 2 p.m. landing. That matches the FAA's written statement. It quoted Mayor Levine Cava on five dead and five injured. It quoted Amazon spokesperson Kelly Nantel naming 21 Air as the operator.<Cite s="abc-miami-cargo-crash" /><Cite s="faa-statements-2026-09-06" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="nbc-miami-cargo-crash" kind="checked">
          <p><strong>NBC</strong> matched the FAA on the flight number, type, origin, and time. It quoted Sheriff Rosie Cordero-Stutz on Runway 30 and vehicles, and Jadallah on three people in critical condition, two others hospitalized, a complex engine fire, and the crew trapped in the airplane. Those quotes are the outlet's record of Sunday's local briefing; they are not in the fire department's saved posts.<Cite s="nbc-miami-cargo-crash" /><Cite s="mdfr-x-update2-2026-09-06" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="npr-miami-cargo-crash" kind="checked">
          <p><strong>NPR</strong> quoted the FAA and the Miami-Dade Aviation Department in line with the saved posts. It quoted the sheriff saying the plane was "operated by Amazon." The FAA's written statement names 21 Air. Amazon's spokesperson, in the same NPR story, said 21 Air was flying it.<Cite s="npr-miami-cargo-crash" passage="the plane, operated by Amazon" /><Cite s="faa-statements-2026-09-06" passage="21 Air Flight 7598" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="cbs-miami-cargo-ntsb" kind="checked">
          <p><strong>CBS</strong>, after Monday's briefing, reported Homendy on Runway 30, the third flight of the day, 1,300 feet past the pavement, the 2012 van and the Toyota, two crew on board, and both recorders recovered. The van year checks out against the transcript. Reuters reports the same core facts. CNBC matches on distance and vehicles and is wrong on the van year.<Cite s="cbs-miami-cargo-ntsb" /><Cite s="cspan-ntsb-briefing-transcript-2026-09-07" passage="2012 white Ford Econoline van" /><Cite s="reuters-miami-cargo-ntsb" /><Cite s="cnbc-miami-cargo-ntsb" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="ap-miami-cargo-crash" kind="checked">
          <p><strong>The Associated Press</strong>, writing Monday morning before the briefing, still said it was not clear whether the pilots survived. Homendy's briefing later that day, as reported by Reuters, said the two people on the airplane survived. AP's Sunday injury split (three critical, two less severe) matches NBC's quote of Jadallah. AP's count of more than 160 cancellations and nearly 325 delays, attributed to FlightAware, is a single-outlet operational count with a timestamp: late Sunday. AP's sentence that arresting beds are "installed at more than 120 airports" is wrong on the unit: the FAA counts 122 beds at 70 airports. AP's Tuesday story, after the sheriff's briefing, has the names, the van, the pilots' release, and more than 225 cancellations over two days.<Cite s="ap-miami-cargo-crash" passage="It wasn’t immediately clear if the pilots survived" /><Cite s="reuters-miami-cargo-ntsb" passage="The two people aboard the plane, the pilot and co-pilot, survived" /><Cite s="ap-miami-cargo-crash" passage="installed at more than 120 airports" /><Cite s="faa-safety-briefing-2026-03" passage="EMAS installations at 70 U.S. airports." /><Cite s="ap-victims-2026-09-08" passage="More than 225 flights were canceled Sunday and Monday" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="local10-sheriff-2026-09-08" kind="checked">
          <p><strong>Local 10</strong>, the Miami ABC affiliate, carried the sheriff's Tuesday identifications with ages, the two pilots' release, and the three hospital conditions. Its names match AP's, including the corrected spelling of Rodriguez Naranjo. It also reported two of four runways still closed Tuesday.<Cite s="local10-sheriff-2026-09-08" passage="Rolando Aleman Leon, 55; Yoel Rodriguez Naranjo, 53; Julio C. Pineda, 75; Carlos Acosta Fajardo, 53; and Javierkys Reyes Quevedo, 47" /><Cite s="ap-victims-2026-09-08" passage="Rolando Aleman Leon, 55; Yoel Rodriguez Naranjo, 53; Julio C. Pineda, 75; Carlos Acosta Fajardo, 53; and Javierkys Reyes Quevedo, 47" /></p></SourcedBlock>
      </section>

      <section id="claim-check">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">One claim, examined</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Claim checked against the record</h2>

        <SourcedBlock source="faa-statements-2026-09-06" kind="checked">
          <p><strong>The claim:</strong> this was an "Amazon cargo plane," as the transportation secretary and most headlines put it, sometimes written as operated by Amazon. <strong>The record:</strong> three different relationships to one airplane. The FAA registry lists the owner as Andromeda Leasing II LLC. The FAA named 21 Air Flight 7598 and the NTSB named 21 Air LLC as the operator; Homendy, on the transcript, called it Amazon Air Flight 7598, a Boeing 767-33 Alpha, operated by 21 Air LLC based in Miami. 21 Air's own page calls it one of its aircraft. Amazon's spokesperson said an Amazon Air plane operated by 21 Air.<Cite s="duffy-x-7598-2026-09-06" passage="an Amazon cargo plane overran the runway" /><Cite s="faa-registry-n1997a" passage="ANDROMEDA LEASING II LLC" /><Cite s="faa-statements-2026-09-06" passage="21 Air Flight 7598" /><Cite s="21air-statement-2026-09-06" passage="one of our aircraft" /><Cite s="cspan-ntsb-briefing-transcript-2026-09-07" passage="Amazon Air Flight 7598, a Boeing 767-33 Alpha. Which was operated by 21 Air LLC based in Miami" /><Cite s="amazon-update-2026-09-06" passage="an Amazon Air plane operated by 21 Air" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <p data-layer="narrative">So "Amazon cargo plane" checks out as shorthand for whose cargo it carried: Amazon's, on Amazon's flight number. It would be wrong as a statement of who flew it. NPR quoted the sheriff saying the plane was "operated by Amazon"; that skips the airline the FAA and NTSB named, and Homendy said the investigation would look at the contract between the two. Amazon's own spokesperson kept both names.<Cite s="npr-miami-cargo-crash" passage="operated by Amazon" /><Cite s="ntsb-dca26ma352-page" passage="21 Air LLC Flight 7598" /><Cite s="cspan-ntsb-briefing-transcript-2026-09-07" passage="the contractual relationship with Amazon" /><Cite s="amazon-update-2026-09-06" passage="an Amazon Air plane operated by 21 Air" /></p>

        <SourcedBlock source="cspan-ntsb-briefing-transcript-2026-09-07" kind="checked">
          <p><strong>A second claim:</strong> the van's model year. Homendy said "2012 white Ford Econoline van." CBS wrote 2012; that checks out. CNBC wrote "2021 white Ford Econoline van." That is wrong.<Cite s="cspan-ntsb-briefing-transcript-2026-09-07" passage="2012 white Ford Econoline van" /><Cite s="cbs-miami-cargo-ntsb" passage="2012 white Ford Econoline van" /><Cite s="cnbc-miami-cargo-ntsb" passage="2021 white Ford Econoline van" /></p></SourcedBlock>
      </section>

      <section id="what-happened-next">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">Monday</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happened next</h2>

        <SourcedBlock source="ntsb-x-briefing-notice-2026-09-07" kind="record">
          <p>The NTSB held a media briefing at 4 p.m. Eastern on Monday at Miami International Airport, Concourse J. C-SPAN recorded that briefing and carries a transcript of it.<Cite s="ntsb-x-briefing-notice-2026-09-07" passage="NTSB will hold a media briefing today at 4 p.m. ET" /><Cite s="cspan-ntsb-briefing-transcript-2026-09-07" passage="Amazon Air Flight 7598, a Boeing 767-33 Alpha" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="cspan-ntsb-briefing-transcript-2026-09-07" kind="record">
          <p>Homendy said the flight data recorder and cockpit voice recorder had been recovered and were going to NTSB headquarters. She described the scene as "utter devastation." She said investigators would look at flight-crew experience and training, 21 Air policies, the contractual relationship with Amazon, and FAA oversight of the operators involved. She said they would not determine probable cause while still on scene.<Cite s="cspan-ntsb-briefing-transcript-2026-09-07" passage="Utter devastation" /><Cite s="cspan-ntsb-briefing-transcript-2026-09-07" passage="the flight crew experience, training. 21 Air LLC policies and procedures, their relationship, the contractual relationship with Amazon. We'll also look at FAA oversight of all involved operators" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="ntsb-x-wreckage-2026-09-07" kind="record">
          <p>After the briefing the NTSB posted a photograph of investigators on the wreckage field, naming Runway 30 and 21 Air LLC Flight 7598.<Cite s="ntsb-x-wreckage-2026-09-07" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="iflymia-x-monday-2026-09-07" kind="record">
          <p>Monday morning the airport said two of four runways remained closed, and that cancellations from Sunday were still leaving aircraft and crews short.<Cite s="iflymia-x-monday-2026-09-07" passage="Two of MIA’s four runways remain closed this morning." /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <p data-layer="narrative">An arresting bed is a strip of crushable material past the runway end, built to stop a plane that leaves the pavement. Homendy said investigators would look at the lack of one on Runway 30, and CBS reported she would ask whether one should have been installed and whether it could have made a difference. Two different questions get asked about the bed that is not there, and they do not have the same answer. The first is compliance. The FAA's newsroom page says a runway safety area can be 500 feet wide and often extends 1,000 feet beyond each runway end, and that beds are used where achieving that full area is not practicable, usually for want of land. The FAA's blog says the same thing in its own words. Experts told AP that Miami appears to meet the federal rule without a bed because it has a 1,000-foot safety area past the runway. So the absence of a bed is not on its face a violation. That is an expert reading, not an FAA finding about Runway 30, and no such finding has been published.<Cite s="cspan-ntsb-briefing-transcript-2026-09-07" passage="the lack thereof of an engineered materials arresting system or EMASS" /><Cite s="cbs-miami-cargo-ntsb" passage="whether an Engineered Materials Arresting System, known as EMAS, should have been installed" /><Cite s="faa-newsroom-emas" passage="often extend 1,000 feet beyond each end of the runway" /><Cite s="faa-newsroom-emas" passage="it is not practicable to achieve the full standard RSA" /><Cite s="faa-blog-runway-safety" passage="Airports install EMAS when they cannot feasibly meet the FAA" /><Cite s="ap-victims-2026-09-08" passage="appears to be compliant with federal rules because it has a 1,000-foot (305-meter) safety buffer" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">The second question is sufficiency, and compliance does not answer it. The FAA's newsroom page states the design condition plainly: a standard installation "will stop most aircraft overrunning the runway at 70 knots (approximately 80 miles per hour)." The FAA's engineering page puts the same number as {emasDesign.stopKnots} knots or less. Flightradar24's tracking data has this airplane leaving the usable runway at {adsb.exitKnots} knots, which is above that design speed and is not a figure the NTSB has checked. So a bed cannot be assumed to have stopped this airplane, and it cannot be assumed to have made no difference either. Homendy, asked both questions at the briefing, said they would be a key part of the investigation.<Cite s="faa-newsroom-emas" passage="A standard EMAS installation will stop most aircraft overrunning the runway at 70 knots (approximately 80 miles per hour)." /><Cite s="faa-emas" passage="A standard EMAS will bring a runway's critical aircraft to a complete stop when it enters the EMAS at 70 knots or less." /><Cite s="fr24-21air-7598-miami" passage="speed at 112 knots as it exited the usable area of the runway" /><Cite s="cspan-ntsb-briefing-transcript-2026-09-07" passage="That will be a key part of our investigation." /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">The national counts are worth stating exactly, because the unit is where coverage went wrong. The FAA's magazine counts {emasCount.installations} installations at {emasCount.airports} U.S. airports; the FAA's blog counts the same {emasCount.installations} by runway end, at the same {emasCount.airports} airports, across more than two dozen states. On how often the beds have worked, two FAA pages disagree with each other: the newsroom page says {emasDesign.arrests} overrunning aircraft stopped carrying {emasDesign.arrestsAboard} people, and the blog says {emasDesign.blogArrests} carrying {emasDesign.blogAboard} since the first bed in 1996. Neither page is dated, and this page does not pick between them.<Cite s="faa-safety-briefing-2026-03" passage="EMAS installations at 70 U.S. airports." /><Cite s="faa-blog-runway-safety" passage="EMAS is now at 122 runway ends at 70 airports in more than two dozen U.S. states." /><Cite s="faa-newsroom-emas" passage="EMAS safely stopped 26 overrunning aircraft carrying 497 crew and passengers" /><Cite s="faa-blog-runway-safety" passage="The system has stopped 22 overrunning aircraft carrying 432 crew and passengers since then." /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Which leaves the question the crash actually poses, and it is narrower than compliance: given the speed the airplane left the pavement at, and given the people, the road, and the fences beyond the runway, was the safety area as designed adequate for people outside it, and would a different arresting arrangement have reduced what happened to them. The NTSB team looking at airport survival factors is the group that will take that up. No answer exists yet.<Cite s="cspan-ntsb-briefing-transcript-2026-09-07" passage="airport survival factors and emergency response" /><Cite s="fr24-21air-7598-miami" passage="speed at 112 knots as it exited the usable area of the runway" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">The public clues about the landing sit in different records, and not one of them is a cause. Set out as the questions investigators will need to determine, they are these.<Cite s="cspan-ntsb-briefing-transcript-2026-09-07" passage="We will not be determining probable cause while we are on scene." /></p>

        <ul data-layer="fact">
          <li>The wind at touchdown, including the gust and any tailwind component. The airport's observation at 1:53 p.m. reported a thunderstorm and wind from 190 degrees at 17 knots gusting 26; what the airplane actually met is a different number, and a meteorology specialist is on the team.<Cite s="noaa-metar-kmia-2026-09-06" passage="METAR KMIA 061753Z 19017G26KT 10SM TS" /><Cite s="cspan-ntsb-briefing-transcript-2026-09-07" passage="we have a meteorologist, specialist here that will begin looking at forecast and weather conditions on landing" /></li>
          <li>The runway's surface condition and any braking reports. Wet or dry is not established, and the NTSB's May 2026 work found airplanes stopping later than their braking assumptions predicted on wet runways.<Cite s="ntsb-dca25sr002-page" passage="11 runway overrun accidents and incidents from a 15-year period, 2008–2022, that occurred after the airplanes involved landed on wet runways" /></li>
          <li>Whether the approach was stable. CBS says flight tracking data it obtained shows that four miles out "the plane suddenly leveled out and stopped its descent for about 25 seconds," leaving the airplane too high, and that it then descended at around twice the normal rate. That is CBS reading tracking data, not an NTSB finding.<Cite s="cbs-21air-safety-record-2026-09-08" passage="the plane suddenly leveled out and stopped its descent for about 25 seconds" /><Cite s="cbs-21air-safety-record-2026-09-08" passage="The deviation meant the plane approached the airport while still too high. Instead of calling off the landing, it then took a steep decline around twice the normal rate." /></li>
          <li>Where the airplane touched down and how fast. Miles O'Brien, watching public video, put it at about 185 miles an hour about halfway down the runway; Homendy said the board has speed data from various sources and is holding it until the recorder confirms it.<Cite s="pbs-miami-cargo-what-went-wrong" passage="They landed at about 185 miles an hour" /><Cite s="cspan-ntsb-briefing-transcript-2026-09-07" passage="We do have data, but it's from various sources, and we want to confirm that data with the flight data recorder" /></li>
          <li>Whether a go-around was called for under the company's own policy. A reporter asked why the crew did not go around; Homendy said the crew has to be interviewed first, and that no interviews had been done.<Cite s="cspan-ntsb-briefing-transcript-2026-09-07" passage="We'll have to interview the crew." /><Cite s="cspan-ntsb-briefing-transcript-2026-09-07" passage="We have not conducted interviews yet." /></li>
          <li>The landing weight and the performance calculation behind it. A performance specialist is on the team.<Cite s="cspan-ntsb-briefing-transcript-2026-09-07" passage="We'll look at aircraft performance." /></li>
          <li>Whether the brakes, tires, spoilers and thrust reversers did what they should. A systems team is examining the airplane's hydraulic, electrical, navigational and pneumatic components, and a separate team the engines.<Cite s="cspan-ntsb-briefing-transcript-2026-09-07" passage="They'll they'll examine the components of the airplane's hydraulic, electrical, navigational, pneumatic, and associated systems" /></li>
          <li>The crew's experience, training, duty time and rest. Homendy named flight crew experience and training as the first thing the operations group would look at.<Cite s="cspan-ntsb-briefing-transcript-2026-09-07" passage="look at the flight crew experience, training" /></li>
          <li>What air traffic control told the crew, and whether the crew got timely weather and runway information. Asked exactly that, Homendy said it is something the board looks at in any investigation and that there is no evidence on it yet.<Cite s="cspan-ntsb-briefing-transcript-2026-09-07" passage="That will be something that we look at. We look at that as part of any investigation, but we don't have evidence to that yet." /></li>
          <li>What the crews who landed just before reported. Homendy said the board wants to talk to them.<Cite s="cspan-ntsb-briefing-transcript-2026-09-07" passage="we're going to want to talk to crew members of flights that may have landed prior to this flight" /></li>
          <li>The airplane's maintenance history, its cargo-conversion paperwork, any deferred items, and the discrepancies the earlier crews that day wrote up. This was the airplane's third flight of the day.<Cite s="cspan-ntsb-briefing-transcript-2026-09-07" passage="This was the 3rd flight for the aircraft." /><Cite s="fr24-21air-7598-miami" passage="when it was converted to cargo configuration" /></li>
        </ul>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Watching: whether the NTSB posts a preliminary report or releases the public docket, whether it releases speed from the flight data recorder (Homendy, as quoted by CBS, wanted that confirmation first), and whether the FAA says in writing whether Runway 30 met the safety-area rule without an arresting bed.<Cite s="cbs-miami-cargo-ntsb" passage="wants to confirm it against the flight data recorder before releasing it" /><Cite s="ntsb-dca26ma352-docket" passage="The docket for this investigation has not been released." /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="unknowns">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">Open questions</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What we do not know yet</h2>
        <ul data-layer="fact">
          <li>The cause of the overrun. Homendy said the team was still collecting evidence. The NTSB has not posted a probable cause.<Cite s="cbs-miami-cargo-ntsb" passage="Homendy said the NTSB would not be determining probable cause while on scene." /></li>
          <li>Which vehicle each of the three people still in hospital was in. The sheriff named them Tuesday without saying, and the van carried seven people while five died in it.<Cite s="local10-sheriff-2026-09-08" passage="Three who were injured remained hospitalized" /><Cite s="cspan-ntsb-briefing-transcript-2026-09-07" passage="2012 white Ford Econoline van" /></li>
          <li>A written NTSB report. The NTSB has an investigation page and a docket address for this accident, but the press-release index we saved on September 8 carries no release, the docket has not been released, and no preliminary report has been posted. What we have from the NTSB are its posts on X, the Monday briefing transcript, and that investigation page.<Cite s="ntsb-dca26ma352-page" passage="Status: Ongoing" /><Cite s="ntsb-dca26ma352-docket" passage="The docket for this investigation has not been released." /></li>
          <li>The runway's condition at 1:53 p.m. The observation says thunderstorm; an analyst watching the video said it was not raining. Wet or dry changes how far a landing airplane needs to stop, which is the subject of the NTSB's May 2026 overrun recommendations.<Cite s="noaa-metar-kmia-2026-09-06" passage="METAR KMIA 061753Z 19017G26KT 10SM TS" /><Cite s="ntsb-dca25sr002-page" passage="11 runway overrun accidents and incidents from a 15-year period, 2008–2022, that occurred after the airplanes involved landed on wet runways" /></li>
          <li>Whether an engineered materials arresting system at the end of Runway 30 would have changed the result. Homendy said that would be examined. The transcript and the FAA page establish that Runway 30 does not have one; they do not answer what it would have done at the speed this landing left the pavement.<Cite s="cspan-ntsb-briefing-transcript-2026-09-07" passage="the lack thereof of an engineered materials arresting system or EMASS" /><Cite s="faa-emas" passage="A standard EMAS will bring a runway's critical aircraft to a complete stop when it enters the EMAS at 70 knots or less." /></li>
          <li>Why the cleaning van was where it was. Homendy said the van was on the inside of the airport with seven people in it, and that the airplane struck navigational aids and equipment before reaching it. Nothing in the records we hold says whether the van was parked or moving, whether the area was one it was authorized to be in, whether the crew was working or traveling between jobs, or what rules govern vehicles at that spot.<Cite s="cspan-ntsb-briefing-transcript-2026-09-07" passage="van was on the inside of the airport" /><Cite s="cspan-ntsb-briefing-transcript-2026-09-07" passage="Overran runway 30 on landing, struck navigational aids and equipment." /></li>
          <li>Whether anyone had assessed the exposure beyond Runway 30 before this. The airplane crossed the safety area, a perimeter fence, a public road, and a second fence line. Whether the placement of navigation equipment, the road, the fencing, or that parking made people foreseeably reachable by an overrun is a question for the NTSB group on airport survival factors; the records we hold contain no earlier assessment of it.<Cite s="cspan-ntsb-briefing-transcript-2026-09-07" passage="The aircraft then traveled through a perimeter fence and impacted a Toyota Corolla Cross LE." /><Cite s="cspan-ntsb-briefing-transcript-2026-09-07" passage="airport survival factors and emergency response" /></li>
          <li>Whether anything in 21 Air's earlier safety record bears on this flight. Former employees have made allegations in a Labor Department proceeding that has not concluded, management denies them, and CBS's own story says it is not known whether past safety issues had any connection to this crash.<Cite s="cbs-21air-safety-record-2026-09-08" passage="It is not known whether past safety issues had any connection to the Sunday incident in Miami." /></li>
          <li>As of September 8, a search found no written Miami-Dade Fire Rescue release beyond the department's posts on X, no 21 Air press release beyond the website statement, no Transportation Department written statement on this accident, no Miami airport newsroom post (the homepage notice is the airport's web record), no hospital records, no cargo totals, no airport passenger count, no Tuesday briefing time in the NTSB posts we saved, and no prior fatal accident on Runway 30 in the records we hold.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

